body:
The use case being described is for specifying files either via `<input type=files>` or a drag and drop operation and read the file contents, even if it is being updated in real time by another process or changes have been made in another app. This is may be needed in apps reading text based files such as CSV in real time, or monitoring log files.

It also makes a case for unifying certain methods of `<input type=files>` or a drag and drop operation. The former currently returns a FileList (that fails to read updates with net::ERR_UPLOAD_FILE_CHANGED or file has been modified error) while the latter a FileEntry type file handle (that successfully reads updates without having to select the file again each time).

This references issues discussed off topic here: https://github.com/WICG/file-system-access/issues/157, https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1326031, https://bugs.chromium.org/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=138987, https://bugs.chromium.org/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=1084880&q=ERR_UPLOAD_FILE_CHANGED&can=2 and also cited in https://github.com/WICG/entries-api/issues/15, issue 4 https://wicg.github.io/entries-api/#issue-4cc238bb "In Chrome, webkitEntries is only populated as the result of a drag-and-drop operation, not when the element is clicked. Should we fix this so it is always populated?"

```
// get the input file as an fileEntry (aka file handle)
const fileEntry = event.target.files[0].webkitGetAsEntry();
```

The DataTransfer constructor may accept addition of input selected files, but fails to return the FileEntry:
```
event.preventDefault();  

const dt = new DataTransfer();  
var file = event.target.files[0];
dt.items.add(file);
console.log(dt.items[0]);
var fileentry = dt.items[0].webkitGetAsEntry();
console.log(fileentry);
```
